  "worked_solution": "To apply the ratio test we define ${a}_{n}={2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}.$ Then, we calculate the ratio of two successive terms and take the limit as $n\\to \\infty :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{a}_{n+1}}{{a}_{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }|\\frac{{2}^{n+1}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n+1}}{{2}^{n}{(x-\\frac{1}{2})}^{n}}| \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }2|x-\\frac{1}{2}| \\\\  & =2|x-\\frac{1}{2}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe series converges if $L<1.$ In this case, the convergence condition is satisfied if $|x-\\frac{1}{2}|<\\frac{1}{2}.$ Therefore, we conclude that the radius of convergence is $R=\\frac{1}{2}.$",
